00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.040
Great, now let’s talk about how&nbsp;
and why “masked autoencoders are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.520
[indeed] scalable vision learners”:

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.760
Hey, Ms. Coffee Bean, take it slow.&nbsp;
First: what is an autoencoder?

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:26.880
Autoencoders back in the day, were reconstructing&nbsp;
the input while being constrained to encode the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.240
input into a lower dimensional space,&nbsp;
then reconstruct the input again.&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.680
enforced by an L2 loss, a simple&nbsp;
distance metric between input and output.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.560
And because of the bottleneck in the middle&nbsp;
reducing the dimensionality of the data,&nbsp;

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:47.520
the hidden space is a compressed,&nbsp;
meaningfully packed, and efficiently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.120
packed version of the input, or in other&nbsp;
words, ideally, the data manifold is learned.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.320
So ideally, semantically similar&nbsp;
points are close to each other in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.760
space. And Autoencoders worked well in computer&nbsp;
vision where the image space is continuous&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.720
but were not as successful in NLP.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:13.200
One of the reasons is the discrete nature of text.&nbsp;
Even if we often pretend that text isn’t discrete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:19.280
by representing words as vectors in word&nbsp;
embeddings, the in-betweenness of words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.160
the interpolation of (especially&nbsp;
context-less) words is often not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.480
that straightforward in the same&nbsp;
sense it is for the visual modality.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:33.600
And this is generating problems when trying&nbsp;
to learn the data manifold in the code space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.840
This then further causes problems&nbsp;
when computing a continuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.760
reconstruction loss in what&nbsp;
is naturally a discrete space.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:47.440
But then everything changed with the introduction&nbsp;
of transformers doing masked autoencoding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:53.680
which happened first for NLP: Think about BERT,&nbsp;
which is doing what a masked autoencoder does:&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:59.040
it corrupts a portion of the data,&nbsp;
usually 15% of the tokens and BERT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:06.240
learns to predict the missing content
by classifying which of all 30k or more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.000
tokens in the English vocabulary could have&nbsp;
been the right word instead of the MASK token.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.240
And we would like to do this autoencoding also&nbsp;
for visual data, because think about it this way:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:25.920
A system that encodes data to decode it again,&nbsp;
not only has the advantage of self-supervision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.960
that you do not need labelled data, because&nbsp;
you just take the input and reconstruct it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.680
but it also learns more general&nbsp;
representations of that modality:

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:41.680
Because self-supervision means that the model&nbsp;
is not allowed to forget everything that is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.680
relevant for the label of that data sample&nbsp;
as it is the case in supervised learning,&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.920
but needs to remember the “essence” of that data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.880
point required to reconstruct&nbsp;
the entire data sample.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.880
Only, you see, masked autoencoding which since&nbsp;
BERT and its likes was made easy for NLP, is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.640
hard for Computer Vision: The authors highlight&nbsp;
three key differences between vision and language.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:12.640
The visual backbones used as encoders for&nbsp;
autoencoders, were not ready yet in the era of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:19.200
CNNs: convolutional-based backbones rely on&nbsp;
the data regularity of image grids and it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.360
not easy to mask things out without introducing&nbsp;
artefacts in the outputs of the convolutions.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:30.080
Secondly, vision and language are very&nbsp;
different in terms of information density.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.720
A word is already a second-order abstraction of&nbsp;
the world and compactly summarizes what otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:43.120
is represented in many pixels in an image. Also,&nbsp;
information in images is much more smeared out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.480
because the neighborhood of some pixels&nbsp;
speak very much about the masked out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:53.440
thing. If we do not see the eyes of&nbsp;
a dog, we still recognize the dog.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.640
Whereas in language, the context is not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:02.960
verbose and the crux of the information is&nbsp;
mostly in the mask token. What does this mean?

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:08.000
Well, while masking out 15% of&nbsp;
tokens in language is already a lot,&nbsp;

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:13.280
masking out 15% of the pixels is&nbsp;
almost no corruption in images.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:18.720
And thirdly, the decoding procedure is more&nbsp;
complicated for vision than for language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.840
Predicting missing words is arguably easier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.120
because one predicts relatively little&nbsp;
things with high semantic information.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.440
For this, just a multi-layer&nbsp;
perceptron, or short: MLP suffices.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.600
But when predicting pixels, one&nbsp;
must regress these many values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:41.280
and while caring about maintaining&nbsp;
contextual harmony between the many pixels.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.880
One pixel alone carries&nbsp;
little semantic information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.560
but the whole neighbourhod of pixels, means a lot.&nbsp;
Therefore, it is not straightforward to do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.880
whole autoencoding with transformers on images, as&nbsp;
it is on text where the output is more compressed.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:02.560
Or well, it was not straightforward until this&nbsp;
paper presented an elegant, simple solution:&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:09.120
The recipe is: Take the image and&nbsp;
divide it into non-overlapping patches.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:16.400
These could be something like 16x16 patches.&nbsp;
So far, we are exactly like in the ViT setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:22.400
and we will stay in the ViT setting where&nbsp;
every patch is transformed into one vector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.440
even if it is not visualized right now in&nbsp;
this video. But each patch here is a vector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:32.720
To understand the prerequisites, please see&nbsp;
our previous videos on the Swin Transformer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.680
ViT and DeiT if you are not familiar&nbsp;
with vision transformers in general.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:43.040
But now, instead of taking all these patch&nbsp;
vectors as input, it’s random masking time!&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:45.680
And by masking, it’s actually meant "removing".

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:53.680
Let’s say 75% of the original number of patches&nbsp;
are removed. Great. Then the authors take the ViT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.920
encoder and encode only these remaining patches.

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:02.560
But why did they remove the patches,&nbsp;
instead of just masking them? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.560 --> 00:08:06.160
because the authors are using&nbsp;
a transformer as an encoder.

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.400
They could have taken all image tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:12.720
including the masked out ones bearing&nbsp;
the value of the [MASK] token,

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.200
but that would blow up the sequence length,
thus compute time and memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.240
because also then also
mask tokens would need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:26.160
be processed in self-attention and then by the&nbsp;
Feed-Forward Layer – in each transformer block.

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:30.240
So because the authors reduced the&nbsp;
sequence length of the image significantly&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.720
by just removing 75% of the&nbsp;
image tokens in the set,&nbsp;

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:40.000
they now can use very large encoders&nbsp;
by increasing the parameter number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.400
in the feedforward networks, greatly&nbsp;
increasing the capacity of the encoder.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:47.440
But Ms. Coffee Bean, they are completely removing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:53.680
intermediate patches like they never were there&nbsp;
before, isn’t that confusing for the encoder?

00:08:53.680 --> 00:09:00.480
No, because these things are almost removed like&nbsp;
they were not there before, because the positional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:06.240
embeddings still remember the fact that this&nbsp;
patch is from the upper part of the image,&nbsp;

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:10.880
this other one from the second row, so&nbsp;
there are many positions missing there.

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:16.800
Thus the structure of the visual modality is&nbsp;
still encoded in the positional embeddings.&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.320
So the encoder with a small sequence length&nbsp;
processes the input and encodes it into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:27.760
latent space. And the output of the encoder&nbsp;
has the same number of tokens as the input has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.920
because the encoder is a transformer and&nbsp;
this is how usually transformers work.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:36.320
But after training, it captures&nbsp;
the image semantics much better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:40.720
Ok, now it is time for decoding,&nbsp;
because we are in an autoencoder,&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:44.400
and this is where the masked&nbsp;
tokens are inserted again.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:50.080
Because now, when reconstructing the original&nbsp;
image, the transformer decoder must follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.520
the transformer architecture: one vector&nbsp;
per token in, one vector per token out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:02.800
So, from the set of the (ideally) semantically&nbsp;
structured latent space tokens and mask tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:05.280
the decoder, which is also a transformer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.480
takes each token and computes something else,&nbsp;
which is ideally the original image patches.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:15.280
Or almost, because to reconstruct&nbsp;
from vectors to the output image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:21.200
the authors again use linear projection to map&nbsp;
the last activations into the pixel space so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.640
that the output of the model is an image and&nbsp;
the mean squared error (MSE) can be computed.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:32.400
It’s important to note, that while the encoder&nbsp;
can be very big in terms of number of parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.880
because its sequence length was significantly&nbsp;
reduced by removing 75% of the tokens,&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:45.840
the decoder operates on all patches, also on&nbsp;
the masked ones, thus needs to be lightweight.

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:52.000
And this was the masked transformer-based&nbsp;
autoencoder! And look, the reconstructions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:56.880
look kind of blurry and Ms. Coffee Bean&nbsp;
thinks that one-two papers further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:02.240
this will be working for higher resolution too,&nbsp;
but you know, the reconstruction quality was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:08.400
the goal of this masked autoencoder anyway,
but the learned representations over here!

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:14.320
Representations that are already rich from&nbsp;
this simple masked autoencoding procedure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:20.160
do not need contrastive learning where it’s&nbsp;
hard to choose good negative examples and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.760
needs image similarities, or complicated&nbsp;
augmentation methods like the competition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:30.000
uses, such as SimCLR, MoCO, DINO, BYOL.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.720
These representations can be used for supervised&nbsp;
tasks too like… ImageNet classification, yay! Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:41.680
Object Detection or semantic segmentation!
For more, check out the paper.

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:45.600
But what you need to know is that for&nbsp;
using the learned representations for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.720
some other supervised task, possibly,&nbsp;
one just has to remove the decoder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:55.120
finetune some more task-specific&nbsp;
layers, which is easy&nbsp;

00:11:55.120 --> 00:12:00.880
but it also needs to run this quite&nbsp;
heavyweight encoder on the unmasked image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:05.280
so on the entire image. Ms. Coffee&nbsp;
Bean, how large are your GPUs again?

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:09.040
Just two more interesting takeaways&nbsp;
and we’re done here, Ms. Coffee Bean:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:15.280
As motivated in the beginning, while 15%&nbsp;
masking in textual data is noisy enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:21.360
for images, masking more is better than&nbsp;
masking less and a masking ratio of 75%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.680
empirically has been shown by&nbsp;
the authors to work very well.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:31.520
Also, random masking is better than regular&nbsp;
masking, which makes sense, because the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:37.600
should have seen all possible combinations of&nbsp;
masks and positional encodings, or positions.

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:42.960
If you want to know even more about all&nbsp;
this, read the paper and other similar ones.

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:51.840
And while leaving, do not&nbsp;
forget to like and subscribe!

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:56.052
Okay, bye!

